Randall (voice), played by Steve Buscemi.

9
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:42,000
Waternoose (voice), played by James Coburn.

10
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:46,000
Celia (voice), played by Jennifer Tilly.

11
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:50,000
Monsters, Inc.

12
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:54,000
(also known as Monsters, Incorporated) is a 2001 American animated comedy film produced by Pixar Animation Studios for Walt Disney
Pictures, and starring the voices of John Goodman, Billy Crystal, Steve Buscemi, James Coburn, Jennifer Tilly, and Mary Gibbs.

13
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:58,000
Directed by Pete Docter, and written by Andrew Stanton and Daniel
Gerson, the film centers on two monsters, the hairy James P.

14
00:42:56,000 --> 00:43:02,000
"Sulley" Sullivan (Goodman) and his one-eyed partner and best friend Mike Wazowski (Crystal), who are employed at
the titular energy-producing factory Monsters, Inc., which generates power by scaring human children.

15
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:06,000
However, the monster world believes that the children are toxic, and when a little human girl, Boo
(Gibbs), sneaks into the factory, the duo must safely return her home while evading discovery.

16
00:49:04,000 --> 00:49:10,000
Docter began developing the film in 1996, following an idea conceived in 1994 when Toy Story (1995) was nearing completion, and
wrote the story with Jill Culton, Jeff Pidgeon and Ralph Eggleston, while Stanton wrote the screenplay with Gerson.

17
00:52:08,000 --> 00:52:14,000
The characters went through many incarnations over the
film's five-year production process.

18
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:18,000
The technical team and animators found new ways to simulate
fur and cloth realistically for the film.

19
00:58:16,000 --> 00:58:22,000
Randy Newman, who composed the music for Pixar's three prior
films, returned to compose the score for its fourth.

20
01:01:20,000 --> 01:01:26,000
Monsters, Inc.

21
01:04:24,000 --> 01:04:30,000
premiered at the El Capitan Theatre in Los Angeles on October 28, 2001, and
was released in theaters in the United States on November 2.

22
01:07:28,000 --> 01:07:34,000
Upon its release, it received critical acclaim and was a commercial success, grossing over
$528 million worldwide to become the third-highest-grossing film of 2001.

23
01:10:32,000 --> 01:10:38,000
The film won the Academy Award for Best Original Song for "If I Didn't Have You" and was nominated for the
inaugural award for Best Animated Feature, as well as Best Original Score and Best Sound Editing.

24
01:13:36,000 --> 01:13:42,000
Monsters, Inc.

25
01:16:40,000 --> 01:16:46,000
saw a 3D re-release in theaters in December 2012.

26
01:19:44,000 --> 01:19:50,000
A prequel titled Monsters University, directed by Dan
Scanlon, was released in June 2013.

27
01:22:48,000 --> 01:22:54,000
A sequel series titled Monsters at Work premiered on Disney+ in July 2021.

28
01:25:52,000 --> 01:25:58,000
The film explores themes of monster, panic, cheating, kidnapping, door.

29
01:28:56,000 --> 01:29:02,000
The tone is lighthearted.
